Run:  python3 book_demo.py              (prints the whole book)
      python3 book_demo.py 3            (prints chapter 3 only)
      python3 book_demo.py search WORD  (finds WORD across chapters)
      python3 book_demo.py html         (writes book.html)
"""
import json, sys, html as _h

BOOK = {'title': 'Lost in the Siberian taiga', 'author': 'Signature original', 'genre': 'Jungle Expeditions', 'chapters': [{'n': 1, 'title': 'The Call of the Wild', 'text': "the guide Kofi was already packed. 'the rival expedition moved yesterday,' the guide Kofi said. 'Full team, heavy gear. If we don't leave tonight, we can read about it in their memoir.'\n\nThe landing was rough — it always is — and the Siberian taiga swallowed them whole: heat, noise, green. the guide Kofi grinned. 'Just like old times.' Riley Fox grinned back. Old times had nearly killed them twice.\n\nBy dawn they were over the Siberian taiga, the green unrolling below like a held breath. Riley Fox checked an ancient map one more time. It hummed faintly, the way true things do when they're near their home."}, {'n': 2, 'title': 'Into the Green', 'text': "The vine nearly ended it: rapids, a broken rope, Riley Fox in the water with an ancient map in a waterproof bag and the guide Kofi's hand the only solid thing in the world. 'Never leave a friend behind,' the guide Kofi gasped. 'Your rule.'\n\nThree days into the Siberian taiga, the trail went cold — and then hot: fresh boot prints, the rival expedition's mark, heading the same way. 'They're a day ahead,' the guide Kofi said, 'but they're heavy. We're fast.'\n\nRiley Fox laughed, coughed river, and lived. That night they found the first marker — an ancient carving that matched an ancient map exactly. The legend was real. It had always been real."}, {'n': 3, 'title': 'The Ruins', 'text': "Inside: wonders. the guide Kofi, the scholar at heart, wept at the murals. Riley Fox kept watch, because wonders in such places usually have teeth. They did: the floor was a trap, the walls were a trap, the air was a trap.\n\nThe ruins of the Siberian taiga were a puzzle built by people who didn't want visitors. an ancient map was the key — literally; it fit a slot in the great door, and the stone sighed open after a thousand years.\n\nthe rival expedition's team hit them in the treasure chamber — of course it was the treasure chamber — and the standoff was pure cinema: an ancient map on its pedestal, two crews, one exit. 'Take it,' the rival expedition said, 'and die rich.'"}, {'n': 4, 'title': 'The Price of Gold', 'text': "The trek out was worse than the trek in: the rival expedition's backup team, the river rising, the guide Kofi feverish from a scratch that turned ugly. Riley Fox carried the guide Kofi and an ancient map in turns, and learned what shoulders are for.\n\nThe backup team wasn't loyal — just paid. When Riley Fox offered them the truth about the rival expedition's promises (empty) versus a share of the legitimate expedition rights (real), they lowered their weapons. Money talks. Truth talks louder.\n\nthe rival expedition was delivered to the authorities with the evidence. an ancient map was delivered to the museum, on loan, with Riley Fox's name on the plaque and the guide Kofi's name right beside it, where it belonged."}, {'n': 5, 'title': 'The Real Treasure', 'text': "curiosity is courage — Riley Fox wrote it in the expedition log, under the date, under the coordinates of the Siberian taiga. Then closed the book. Then opened the new map.\n\nA kid asked Riley Fox if it was worth it — the danger, the lost years. Riley Fox thought of the river, the ruins, the guide Kofi's hand in the rapids. 'The thing,' Riley Fox said, 'was never the thing.'\n\nThey stood before an ancient map one last time. It had started all this: the legend, the race, the ruin, the rescue. Riley Fox saluted it. Some doors, once opened, stay open forever."}, {'n': 6, 'title': 'Next Horizon', 'text': "Engines up. curiosity is courage. The horizon, as always, was just the beginning.\n\nThe plane — or boat, or truck, or camel — was already arranged. It always is, when an ancient map calls. That's the thing about legends: they don't end. They just wait for the next believer.\n\nSomewhere, the rival expedition was reading about it in a cell and planning. Let them. The world is wide, and Riley Fox is faster, and the guide Kofi is smarter, and that's usually enough."}]}

def word_count():
    return sum(len(c["text"].split()) for c in BOOK["chapters"])

def print_book(ch=None):
    print("=" * 64)
    print(BOOK["title"].upper(), "\nby", BOOK["author"], "-", BOOK["genre"])
    print("=" * 64)
    for c in BOOK["chapters"]:
        if ch and c["n"] != ch:
            continue
        print("\n\nCHAPTER %d: %s\n%s" % (c["n"], c["title"], "-" * 40))
        print(c["text"])
    print("\n[%d words, %d chapters — complete]" % (word_count(), len(BOOK["chapters"])))

def search(word):
    w = word.lower()
    for c in BOOK["chapters"]:
        for i, para in enumerate(c["text"].split("\n\n")):
            if w in para.lower():
                print("[ch %d, para %d] %s..." % (c["n"], i + 1, para[:160].replace("\n", " ")))

def write_html(path="book.html"):
    parts = ["<html><head><meta charset='utf-8'><title>%s</title></head><body>" % _h.escape(BOOK["title"])]
    parts.append("<h1>%s</h1><p><i>by %s — %s</i></p>" % (_h.escape(BOOK["title"]), _h.escape(BOOK["author"]), _h.escape(BOOK["genre"])))
    for c in BOOK["chapters"]:
        parts.append("<h2>Chapter %d: %s</h2>" % (c["n"], _h.escape(c["title"])))
        for para in c["text"].split("\n\n"):
            parts.append("<p>%s</p>" % _h.escape(para))
    parts.append("</body></html>")
    open(path, "w").write("\n".join(parts))
    print("wrote", path)

if __name__ == "__main__":
    a = sys.argv[1:]
    if a and a[0] == "html":
        write_html()
    elif len(a) >= 2 and a[0] == "search":
        search(a[1])
    elif a and a[0].isdigit():
        print_book(int(a[0]))
    else:
        print_book()

